1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,986
Text looks like one idea until you store it.

2
00:00:02,986 --> 00:00:05,333
CHAR n is a fixed-width string.

3
00:00:05,333 --> 00:00:11,041
If you declare CHAR four for a currency code, USD always occupies four.

4
00:00:11,041 --> 00:00:13,652
Short codes like that are the rare good use.

5
00:00:13,652 --> 00:00:17,125
VARCHAR n is a string up to n characters.

6
00:00:17,125 --> 00:00:21,250
email VARCHAR two-fifty-four matches a real maximum.

7
00:00:21,250 --> 00:00:23,083
The n is a contract.

8
00:00:23,083 --> 00:00:24,666
It is not a suggestion.

9
00:00:24,666 --> 00:00:30,972
A value that is too long is an error, or it is trimmed, depending on the engine and the mode.

10
00:00:30,972 --> 00:00:34,416
Do not guess fifty because a form looked fifty wide.

11
00:00:34,416 --> 00:00:39,222
TEXT is a string with a large limit, often treated as a document.

12
00:00:39,222 --> 00:00:45,555
SQLite will store any length in TEXT and largely ignore the n in VARCHAR n.

13
00:00:45,555 --> 00:00:49,305
That is a dialect fact, not a reason to skip thinking.

14
00:00:49,305 --> 00:00:55,750
PostgreSQL treats CHAR as padded, VARCHAR as bounded, TEXT as unbounded.

15
00:00:55,750 --> 00:01:03,208
MySQL historically mixed TEXT with size families: TINYTEXT, TEXT, MEDIUMTEXT.

16
00:01:03,208 --> 00:01:07,680
Pick the dialect you are on, then pick the smallest honest bound.

17
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:10,180
Names are VARCHAR, not CHAR.

18
00:01:10,180 --> 00:01:11,972
People have different lengths.

19
00:01:11,972 --> 00:01:15,638
Padding names with spaces will haunt your equality tests.

20
00:01:15,638 --> 00:01:18,736
Codes that are truly fixed width can be CHAR.

21
00:01:18,736 --> 00:01:21,083
country_code CHAR two.

22
00:01:21,083 --> 00:01:23,763
Collation decides how letters compare.

23
00:01:23,763 --> 00:01:28,583
Case insensitive email sounds friendly and then merges two accounts.

24
00:01:28,583 --> 00:01:31,722
Know whether A and a are the same in this column.

25
00:01:31,722 --> 00:01:33,833
Unicode is the default you want.

26
00:01:33,833 --> 00:01:37,805
Do not store names in a bytes encoding you cannot round-trip.

27
00:01:37,805 --> 00:01:39,930
Empty string is not NULL.

28
00:01:39,930 --> 00:01:42,819
NULL means unknown or inapplicable.

29
00:01:42,819 --> 00:01:45,583
Empty string means someone typed nothing.

30
00:01:45,583 --> 00:01:47,236
Those are different facts.

31
00:01:47,236 --> 00:01:51,472
Indexes on huge TEXT columns are expensive or forbidden.

32
00:01:51,472 --> 00:01:56,250
If you search inside a chapter, you want a later tool, not a primary key.

33
00:01:56,250 --> 00:02:00,097
Trim at the edges in the application or with a constraint.

34
00:02:00,097 --> 00:02:04,750
Leading spaces make unique emails that look identical to a human.

35
00:02:04,750 --> 00:02:06,722
Write the type as a sentence.

36
00:02:06,722 --> 00:02:14,694
This column holds a person's name, up to one hundred characters, Unicode, case sensitive, empty not allowed.

37
00:02:14,694 --> 00:02:19,041
Then CHAR, VARCHAR, or TEXT is an easy choice.

38
00:02:19,041 --> 00:02:22,333
Trim, then constrain, then index.

39
00:02:22,333 --> 00:02:26,986
An index on untrimmed email still lets two visual twins in.

40
00:02:26,986 --> 00:02:31,694
Some engines fold case with citext or a functional unique index.

41
00:02:31,694 --> 00:02:36,166
Use those when email identity is case insensitive by policy.

42
00:02:36,166 --> 00:02:39,472
Do not lowercase in some rows and not others.

43
00:02:39,472 --> 00:02:45,527
CHAR is for true codes, not for padding names into a grid that looks neat in a terminal.

44
00:02:45,527 --> 00:02:46,944
Neat is not a type.

45
00:02:46,944 --> 00:02:50,166
National characters historically had NCHAR.

46
00:02:50,166 --> 00:02:54,208
Today you want Unicode in ordinary VARCHAR or TEXT.

47
00:02:54,208 --> 00:02:56,402
Emoji will appear in names.

48
00:02:56,402 --> 00:02:57,458
Plan for it.

49
00:02:57,458 --> 00:03:00,652
Length in characters is not length in bytes.

50
00:03:00,652 --> 00:03:04,180
VARCHAR n in PostgreSQL is characters.

51
00:03:04,180 --> 00:03:07,819
Some MySQL settings historically counted bytes.

52
00:03:07,819 --> 00:03:10,194
Read the manual for the server you have.

53
00:03:10,194 --> 00:03:12,847
Comments and biographies can be TEXT.

54
00:03:12,847 --> 00:03:15,347
Search them with later tools if you must.

55
00:03:15,347 --> 00:03:16,944
Do not primary-key them.

56
00:03:16,944 --> 00:03:21,180
A check on length can back up a VARCHAR bound if you want a clearer error.

57
00:03:21,180 --> 00:03:23,791
Still pick a bound that matches the world.

58
00:03:23,791 --> 00:03:26,597
The sentence you wrote at the start is the spec.

59
00:03:26,597 --> 00:03:29,513
Types are how the spec enters the database.

60
00:03:29,513 --> 00:03:30,805
Keep them aligned.

61
00:03:30,805 --> 00:03:37,027
When they drift, the form on the website will accept what the table refuses, or the other way around.

62
00:03:37,027 --> 00:03:40,680
That drift is a bug in the contract, not in the user.

63
00:03:40,680 --> 00:03:42,347
A type is a fence.

64
00:03:42,347 --> 00:03:46,611
CHAR, VARCHAR, and TEXT are three fence heights.

65
00:03:46,611 --> 00:03:49,541
Codes, names, and documents.

66
00:03:49,541 --> 00:03:52,888
If you only remember one rule, remember padding.

67
00:03:52,888 --> 00:03:54,208
Do not pad a name.

68
00:03:54,208 --> 00:03:57,611
If you remember two, remember empty versus NULL.

69
00:03:57,611 --> 00:04:00,513
If you remember three, remember collation.

70
00:04:00,513 --> 00:04:05,347
Those three surprises cause more support tickets than exotic encodings.

71
00:04:05,347 --> 00:04:10,194
Fence the column, then test equality with the strings you actually receive.

72
00:04:10,194 --> 00:04:14,958
Including emoji, including spaces, including mixed case.

73
00:04:14,958 --> 00:04:16,597
The test is the spec.

74
00:04:16,597 --> 00:04:18,361
Ship the spec in the type.

75
00:04:18,361 --> 00:04:23,444
Type selection happens after entities, attributes, and keys exist.

76
00:04:23,444 --> 00:04:28,972
A string column still needs a fence: how long, how fixed, how empty.

77
00:04:28,972 --> 00:04:34,000
CHAR, VARCHAR, and TEXT are the three fences you will actually choose among.

78
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:35,472
CHAR is fixed length.

79
00:04:35,472 --> 00:04:41,194
CHAR two for a US state code occupies two characters even if you tried to store one.

80
00:04:41,194 --> 00:04:47,902
Short codes, padded, compared as padded, belong in CHAR when the length is truly fixed.

81
00:04:48,026 --> 00:04:49,323
Do not CHAR a name.

82
00:04:49,323 --> 00:04:55,489
Padding turns Maya into Maya plus spaces, and equality becomes a costume party.

83
00:04:55,489 --> 00:04:58,976
VARCHAR is variable length with a declared maximum.

84
00:04:58,976 --> 00:05:03,087
VARCHAR 255 became a habit, not a measurement.

85
00:05:03,087 --> 00:05:09,559
Measure: how long is a legal username, an email, a product name in this business.

86
00:05:09,559 --> 00:05:13,642
Set the max to the business rule, not to a folklore number.

87
00:05:13,642 --> 00:05:17,809
Too small, and a real value is cut off or refused.

88
00:05:17,809 --> 00:05:23,476
Too large, and you waste space and sometimes memory for sorts, depending on the engine.

89
00:05:23,476 --> 00:05:30,295
TEXT is for strings whose length you cannot honestly cap: descriptions, bodies, comments.

90
00:05:30,295 --> 00:05:34,101
Some engines treat long VARCHAR and TEXT similarly.

91
00:05:34,101 --> 00:05:38,031
Some do not index TEXT the same way they index VARCHAR.

92
00:05:38,031 --> 00:05:41,892
Know your engine before you put a TEXT column in a WHERE equals.

93
00:05:41,892 --> 00:05:44,017
Empty string is not NULL.

94
00:05:44,017 --> 00:05:47,059
NULL means unknown or not applicable.

95
00:05:47,059 --> 00:05:49,448
Empty means present and blank.

96
00:05:49,448 --> 00:05:56,767
Decide which one a missing middle name is, and put that decision in the column, not in five application layers.

97
00:05:56,767 --> 00:06:01,962
Collation decides whether a and A are equal, and where ß sorts.

98
00:06:01,962 --> 00:06:08,656
A unique email with a case-insensitive collation treats Maya@ and maya@ as a collision.

99
00:06:08,656 --> 00:06:10,212
That might be what you want.

100
00:06:10,212 --> 00:06:11,198
Say so.

101
00:06:11,198 --> 00:06:14,517
Unicode is not optional if people type names.

102
00:06:14,517 --> 00:06:23,267
Use the character set that can hold the letters you will actually receive, including accented letters and emoji if your product allows them.

103
00:06:23,267 --> 00:06:26,045
Codes, names, documents.

104
00:06:26,045 --> 00:06:28,087
CHAR for codes of known width.

105
00:06:28,087 --> 00:06:31,059
VARCHAR for names and labels with a max.

106
00:06:31,059 --> 00:06:32,698
TEXT for documents.

107
00:06:32,698 --> 00:06:36,226
If you only remember one surprise, remember padding.

108
00:06:36,226 --> 00:06:39,628
If you remember two, remember empty versus NULL.

109
00:06:39,628 --> 00:06:42,531
If you remember three, remember collation.

110
00:06:42,531 --> 00:06:49,281
Test equality with the strings you actually receive, then ship that test as the type and the collation.

111
00:06:49,281 --> 00:06:52,920
Close with questions, the way a careful lecture does.

112
00:06:52,920 --> 00:06:55,976
Answer in a sentence you could defend at a keyboard.

113
00:06:55,976 --> 00:06:56,989
CHAR for?

114
00:06:56,989 --> 00:06:58,489
Fixed width codes.

115
00:06:58,489 --> 00:06:59,656
VARCHAR for?

116
00:06:59,656 --> 00:07:02,351
Variable labels with a measured max.

117
00:07:02,351 --> 00:07:03,378
TEXT for?

118
00:07:03,378 --> 00:07:05,559
Bodies you cannot honestly cap.

119
00:07:05,559 --> 00:07:06,920
Padding surprise?

120
00:07:06,920 --> 00:07:09,823
CHAR names equal only with spaces.

121
00:07:09,823 --> 00:07:11,351
Empty versus NULL?

122
00:07:11,351 --> 00:07:13,670
Blank present versus unknown.

123
00:07:13,670 --> 00:07:14,614
Collation?

124
00:07:14,614 --> 00:07:18,017
Whether a and A collide, and how they sort.

125
00:07:18,017 --> 00:07:20,226
VARCHAR 255?

126
00:07:20,226 --> 00:07:22,253
A habit, not a measurement.

127
00:07:22,253 --> 00:07:23,239
Unicode?

128
00:07:23,239 --> 00:07:25,531
Required if people type names.

129
00:07:25,531 --> 00:07:30,253
If an answer needed a second table, go draw it before you write more SQL.

